WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.540 --> 00:00:06.660
Television is great for telling serialized stories&nbsp;
over time, but not every TV series is meant to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:06.660 --> 00:00:12.240
last. None of the shows on this list made it past&nbsp;
a single season — and we still aren't over it.
 &nbsp;

00:00:12.780 --> 00:00:16.980
Pulling from their own awkward high&nbsp;
school experiences as newcomers and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:16.980 --> 00:00:21.540
outsiders in small communities, David&nbsp;
Guarascio and Moses Port recalled the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:25.440
genesis of their show, Aliens in America,&nbsp;
in an interview with Patheos, saying,

00:00:25.440 --> 00:00:29.100
"We [...] started talking about politics&nbsp;
and the giant gap that exists between&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:29.100 --> 00:00:33.180
Americans and really the rest of the&nbsp;
world, specifically the Muslim world."

00:00:33.180 --> 00:00:39.000
Airing on the CW between 2007 and 2008,&nbsp;
Aliens in America focuses on social misfit&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:39.000 --> 00:00:43.320
Justin Tolchuck and his wholesome yet&nbsp;
quirky Wisconsin family. They take in a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:43.320 --> 00:00:47.820
foreign exchange student from Pakistan for&nbsp;
a culture clash comedy about "peace, love,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.820 --> 00:00:50.580
and understanding," as the show's theme song goes.

00:00:51.360 --> 00:00:54.180
"Why don't you wear a warmer jacket&nbsp;
over that boy blouse of yours."

00:00:54.180 --> 00:00:59.100
When Scott Patterson, who played patriarch&nbsp;
Gary Tolchuck, sat down with IGN in 2008,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:59.100 --> 00:01:02.760
he was full of praise for Aliens&nbsp;
in America's humor and its message,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:02.760 --> 00:01:05.700
calling it relevant for the times and saying,

00:01:05.700 --> 00:01:09.900
"The beauty of the show is that it&nbsp;
shows there's ignorance on both sides."

00:01:09.900 --> 00:01:12.660
Critics were fond of it, too,&nbsp;
even when the ratings didn't&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:12.660 --> 00:01:15.000
back it up. The Boston Herald wrote that,

00:01:15.000 --> 00:01:19.560
"Aliens in America is warm, funny,&nbsp;
and smart. It doesn't stand a chance."

00:01:19.560 --> 00:01:22.620
That prediction proved true. Two years later,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.620 --> 00:01:25.200
Variety was still lamenting&nbsp;
the show's cancellation.

00:01:25.200 --> 00:01:31.260
With the success of Star Wars came imitators&nbsp;
hot on its heels, hoping to turn their own&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.260 --> 00:01:37.320
space dust into magic. In 1978, producer Glen&nbsp;
A. Larson tried to launch his own space opera:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.320 --> 00:01:42.240
Battlestar Galactica. In an interview with&nbsp;
Science and Fantasy Film Classics magazine,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:42.240 --> 00:01:44.760
Larson described his approach to the show:

00:01:44.760 --> 00:01:49.500
"It's Genesis. It's basically the&nbsp;
concept of all the human cultures&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:49.500 --> 00:01:54.300
in space having evolved from a mother&nbsp;
planet culture. [...] We've got a truly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:54.300 --> 00:01:58.200
rare opportunity to really open up&nbsp;
that frontier and talk about what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:58.200 --> 00:02:02.640
could happen out there in space. It doesn't&nbsp;
have to be all robots and flying machines."

00:02:03.380 --> 00:02:09.720
"Yes, the mother world of all&nbsp;
humans. Where life began."

00:02:09.720 --> 00:02:13.920
The series takes place at the end of a&nbsp;
long conflict between humans and Cylons,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:13.920 --> 00:02:19.620
robots created by an extinct reptilian&nbsp;
race. Humanity rallies behind the Galactica,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:19.620 --> 00:02:23.760
a battlestar warship led by Commander&nbsp;
Adama, played by Lorne Greene.

00:02:23.760 --> 00:02:27.720
"Ready. Fire."

00:02:27.720 --> 00:02:31.380
Things got off to a bumpy start&nbsp;
for the show when the premiere was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:31.380 --> 00:02:35.460
interrupted by President Jimmy Carter's&nbsp;
announcement of the Camp David Accords.

00:02:35.460 --> 00:02:37.680
The good fight for humanity raged on,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:41.880
but competition from other networks&nbsp;
and constant comparisons to Star Wars&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:41.880 --> 00:02:47.280
did the show no favors. Ron Cowan from the&nbsp;
wrote of Battlestar Galactica at the time,

00:02:47.280 --> 00:02:52.740
"The opening was impressive, but the show [...]&nbsp;
became more a soap opera than a space opera."

00:02:52.740 --> 00:02:56.160
The writing was on the wall.&nbsp;
Battlestar Galactica was taken&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:56.160 --> 00:03:01.500
out of orbit the following season. Many&nbsp;
prefer the highly acclaimed 2004 reboot,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:01.500 --> 00:03:04.800
but plenty of viewers still have a&nbsp;
soft spot for the original series.

00:03:05.700 --> 00:03:10.920
Out of all the zany characters Martin Short has&nbsp;
brought to life, few have utilized his talents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:10.920 --> 00:03:16.200
better than buttoned-up, triangle-haired,&nbsp;
happy-go lucky Ed Grimley. Short debuted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:16.200 --> 00:03:21.720
the character at The Second City and Grimley was&nbsp;
further developed on SCTV and Saturday Night Live.

00:03:21.720 --> 00:03:25.920
But Short wasn't interested in playing&nbsp;
Grimley on the big screen. Instead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:25.920 --> 00:03:31.140
he took Hanna-Barbera up on an offer to create a&nbsp;
Saturday morning cartoon, The Completely Mental&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:31.140 --> 00:03:36.420
Misadventures of Ed Grimley. The move made sense&nbsp;
because, as he told New York's The Post-Star,

00:03:36.420 --> 00:03:40.200
"It's a real-life person acting&nbsp;
like an animated character."

00:03:40.200 --> 00:03:44.160
They modeled the cartoon after The Adventures&nbsp;
of Rocky and Bullwinkle and Friends,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:44.160 --> 00:03:49.500
making it something both kids and parents&nbsp;
could enjoy. Short went tall on talent,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:49.500 --> 00:03:54.780
bringing Catherine O'Hara, Andrea Martin, and&nbsp;
Jonathan Winters on board to amp the absurdity.

00:03:54.780 --> 00:04:01.140
The following season, Grimley got the ax, replaced&nbsp;
by fellow SCTV alum John Candy's animated show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.140 --> 00:04:06.240
Camp Candy. Short bemoaned the decision in&nbsp;
an interview with The Tampa Tribune, saying,

00:04:06.240 --> 00:04:12.300
"It had reviews like we wrote them ourselves,&nbsp;
and NBC couldn't take that show off fast enough.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:12.300 --> 00:04:17.460
They thought it was too odd. If I had done Ed&nbsp;
Grimley, Boy Scout Leader, it would be on today."

00:04:18.240 --> 00:04:23.460
MC Serch, aka Michael Berrin, knows a thing or&nbsp;
two about what it takes to be respected as a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:23.460 --> 00:04:29.340
white rapper. In 2007, the former 3rd Bass member&nbsp;
hosted Ego Trip's The (White) Rapper Show on VH1.

00:04:29.340 --> 00:04:31.680
"I was confused when I seen the&nbsp;
white rappers comin' down the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.680 --> 00:04:34.200
block. I was wonderin' what was&nbsp;
goin' on, you know what I mean?"

00:04:34.200 --> 00:04:39.600
According to an interview Serch gave to The&nbsp;
Virginian-Pilot, the competition series mixed&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:39.600 --> 00:04:46.260
elements of "Survivor, hip-hop, Jeopardy, racial&nbsp;
tension, political views, and Seinfeld" into one.

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.000
Ten hopeful rappers with stage&nbsp;
names like $hamrock, Jus Rhyme,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.000 --> 00:04:57.960
Persia, G-Child, and 100 Proof — lived in the&nbsp;
Bronx and battled for $100,000. They got to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:57.960 --> 00:05:03.720
work with hip-hop royalty like Kurtis Blow,&nbsp;
Prince Paul, Kool Keith, Grandmaster Flash,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:03.720 --> 00:05:08.760
and House of Pain members Danny Boy O'Connor&nbsp;
and Erik Francis Schrody, aka Everlast.

00:05:08.760 --> 00:05:12.000
One contestant, Jon Boy, told The Virginian-Pilot:

00:05:12.000 --> 00:05:15.900
"I felt like the show was rap&nbsp;
boot camp. It was really humbling,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:15.900 --> 00:05:18.120
trying to educate us on where hip-hop came from."

00:05:18.900 --> 00:05:23.220
Serch was hopeful that a second season would&nbsp;
get greenlit, but it never materialized.

00:05:23.820 --> 00:05:29.520
Folk comedy duo Riki Lindhome and Kate Micucci&nbsp;
are better known to fans as Garfunkel and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:29.520 --> 00:05:33.120
Oates. They're sometimes referred to&nbsp;
as the female Flight of the Conchords,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:33.120 --> 00:05:37.440
which they don't mind. In an interview with&nbsp;
the Tampa Bay Times, Lindhome admitted,

00:05:37.440 --> 00:05:41.460
"We describe ourselves as that because it's&nbsp;
an easy way to let people know what we do."

00:05:41.460 --> 00:05:46.980
Their naughty, sharp-tongued brand of musical&nbsp;
humor led HBO to develop a pilot with them,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:46.980 --> 00:05:51.720
but it never took flight. Instead, they&nbsp;
landed at IFC with a self-titled show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:51.720 --> 00:05:54.780
directed and executive produced by Fred Savage.

00:05:54.780 --> 00:05:59.040
"We can't open for our own knock-off. That's like&nbsp;
Paul McCartney opening for a Beatles cover band."

00:05:59.040 --> 00:06:01.380
Playing exaggerated versions of themselves,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:01.380 --> 00:06:05.460
Lindhome and Micucci set about trying&nbsp;
to make it big in Hollywood. The duo&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:05.460 --> 00:06:10.320
had to tone down their act to comply with the&nbsp;
TV-14 rating. Micucci told Under the Radar,

00:06:10.320 --> 00:06:15.060
"Because the show's TV-14, a lot of our&nbsp;
dirtier material we were not able to use."

00:06:15.060 --> 00:06:22.020
Instead, they poked fun at everything from Sesame&nbsp;
Street and Saved by the Bell to sports fans and,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:22.020 --> 00:06:27.540
of course, themselves. Joining in on the fun were&nbsp;
the likes of Tig Notaro, Kevin Pollak, 'Weird Al'&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:27.540 --> 00:06:33.480
Yankovic, Kumail Nanjiani, Ben Kingsley, and even&nbsp;
rocker John Oates, who told Entertainment Weekly,

00:06:33.480 --> 00:06:35.340
"I thought they were really clever and funny."

00:06:35.340 --> 00:06:40.860
So did critics. The show dropped to&nbsp;
rave reviews in 2014. News of the show's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:40.860 --> 00:06:45.900
cancellation after a single season didn't go&nbsp;
down well. As one AV Club headline put it:

00:06:45.900 --> 00:06:50.340
"IFC stops being cool, cancels&nbsp;
'Garfunkel And Oates.'"

00:06:51.000 --> 00:06:56.760
It's hard to fathom that The Honeymooners, one of&nbsp;
the most iconic sitcoms of all time, lasted just a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:56.760 --> 00:07:03.480
single season. The show that CBS first beamed onto&nbsp;
black-and-white TV sets in 1955 set the standard&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:03.480 --> 00:07:09.000
for television comedy for decades, influencing&nbsp;
everything from The Flintstones to King of Queens.

00:07:09.000 --> 00:07:14.280
The show originated as a sketch on Jackie&nbsp;
Gleason's variety show Cavalcade of Stars in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.280 --> 00:07:20.160
1951. The domestic antics of Gleason's bus driver&nbsp;
character, Ralph Kramden, followed Gleason to The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:20.160 --> 00:07:24.060
Jackie Gleason Show and would eventually&nbsp;
become the basis for a half hour comedy.

00:07:24.060 --> 00:07:25.920
"I don't know anybody does the mambo.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:25.920 --> 00:07:31.140
I don't do it. Norton doesn't do&nbsp;
it. My grandmother never did it."

00:07:31.140 --> 00:07:33.900
Although The Honeymooners trailed&nbsp;
The Perry Como Show in the ratings,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:33.900 --> 00:07:39.300
CBS was hungry for more. Alas, Gleason&nbsp;
turned them down. The star is quoted&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:39.300 --> 00:07:43.980
in William J. Weatherby's Jackie Gleason: An&nbsp;
Intimate Portrait of the Great One as saying,

00:07:43.980 --> 00:07:46.980
"They wouldn't believe me when I&nbsp;
said we couldn't come up with the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:46.980 --> 00:07:49.380
same high quality of scripts for a second year."

00:07:50.040 --> 00:07:52.860
"Ralph, how could you do such a thing?"

00:07:52.860 --> 00:07:56.040
"Humina humina humina –"

00:07:56.040 --> 00:08:01.560
Gleason explained that he, quote, "wanted to go&nbsp;
out on top." The Honeymooners characters would&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:01.560 --> 00:08:06.960
live on in specials and variety shows but&nbsp;
nothing would compare to those 39 episodes.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:06.960 --> 00:08:11.820
Gleason was nominated for an Emmy for his&nbsp;
portrayal of Ralph Kramden in the show. Did&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:11.820 --> 00:08:16.440
he ever regret not making more? Co-star Art&nbsp;
Carney confided to the Corsicana Daily Sun,

00:08:16.440 --> 00:08:20.160
"I think Gleason wishes he made 139 of them."

00:08:20.760 --> 00:08:26.100
One of the best kids' game shows ever&nbsp;
made has been mostly forgotten to time.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:26.100 --> 00:08:31.500
I'm Telling ran on Saturday mornings for&nbsp;
a single season between 1987 and 1988.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:31.500 --> 00:08:34.740
Capitalizing on the popularity&nbsp;
of Nickelodeon's Double Dare,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:34.740 --> 00:08:38.700
producer Haim Saban came up with the&nbsp;
idea. He told the Los Angeles Times:

00:08:38.700 --> 00:08:42.540
"We were looking for creative ways to&nbsp;
produce something different for children."

00:08:42.540 --> 00:08:45.720
Saban added that there was an overabundance of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:45.720 --> 00:08:50.400
animated programming and his show was far&nbsp;
less expensive than a cartoon would be.

00:08:50.400 --> 00:08:55.380
The format was similar to The Newlywed Game,&nbsp;
but here it was siblings who'd guess how the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:55.380 --> 00:09:00.360
other would answer a revealing question. The&nbsp;
winning pair advanced to the final round: the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:00.360 --> 00:09:05.580
Pick-A-Prize Arcade. Host Laurie Faso talked about&nbsp;
the show with the Detroit Free Press, saying,

00:09:05.580 --> 00:09:08.220
"It's not demeaning to kids. It's not educational,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:08.220 --> 00:09:12.300
but there's no violence in it and it's&nbsp;
not going to hurt kids in any way."

00:09:12.300 --> 00:09:16.200
There were a couple of celebrity&nbsp;
episodes of I'm Telling. In one,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:16.200 --> 00:09:20.760
Sean Astin appeared alongside his brother.&nbsp;
Other episodes featured then-unknown talents&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:20.760 --> 00:09:25.380
like Paul Walker, Lindsay Price, and&nbsp;
Giovanni Ribisi. If you can find it,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:25.380 --> 00:09:29.400
you can relive the magic of I'm Telling&nbsp;
by scooping up a copy of the board game.

00:09:30.000 --> 00:09:36.180
Importing a beloved British show is always a&nbsp;
risky proposition. For every The Office, there&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:36.180 --> 00:09:41.280
are dozens of failures. But Arrested Development&nbsp;
writer/producer Brad Copeland saw potential in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.280 --> 00:09:46.080
the raunchy U.K. comedy The Inbetweeners, a&nbsp;
show about four teens who aren't as hip as&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:46.080 --> 00:09:52.140
they'd like to be. Beginning in 2012, MTV rolled&nbsp;
out 12 episodes, some directed by Taika Waititi.

00:09:52.140 --> 00:09:54.540
"We're gonna go on out there, and&nbsp;
we're gonna cause some havoc."

00:09:54.540 --> 00:09:58.800
Critics and fans of the original weren't&nbsp;
willing to give it a chance to stand on&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:58.800 --> 00:10:02.940
its own, and new viewers perhaps didn't&nbsp;
know what to make of it. But the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:02.940 --> 00:10:07.500
version produced its own brand of laughter,&nbsp;
especially when Zack Pearlman was involved.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:07.500 --> 00:10:11.700
Like the imported Skins before it, the&nbsp;
Inbetweeners remake never caught on in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:11.700 --> 00:10:16.140
America and soon got the ax. Pearlman&nbsp;
penned a goodbye on Tumblr, saying,

00:10:16.140 --> 00:10:20.940
"I will always be able to look back at and&nbsp;
smile knowing that we did a fantastic job."

00:10:20.940 --> 00:10:23.400
According to Joe Thomas of the U.K. version,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:23.400 --> 00:10:27.480
the American version was doomed from the&nbsp;
start as he explained to Sunday Brunch,

00:10:27.480 --> 00:10:31.440
"They couldn't really swear very much&nbsp;
because it was on network TV. And&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.440 --> 00:10:35.040
then you couldn't show them drinking and&nbsp;
stuff and that's kind of the whole show."

00:10:35.040 --> 00:10:37.500
"Why do you smell like vomit and cheap alcohol?"

00:10:37.500 --> 00:10:40.260
"It wasn't cheap. It had gold in it."

00:10:41.160 --> 00:10:46.140
In 2005, the Globe and Mail pointed out&nbsp;
that three major networks would, quote,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:46.140 --> 00:10:51.420
"air new dramas about the United States under&nbsp;
attack from outside forces" that fall. NBC's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:51.420 --> 00:10:57.000
effort was Surface; CBS had Threshold; and&nbsp;
ABC went with the unidentified frightening&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:57.000 --> 00:11:02.220
objects of Invasion. In an interview with Today,&nbsp;
Invasion creator Shaun Cassidy called the show,

00:11:02.220 --> 00:11:04.980
"A suspense thriller in the bright sunlight."

00:11:04.980 --> 00:11:10.260
Invasion takes place in the Everglades of Florida.&nbsp;
The story kicks off after a devastating hurricane,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:10.260 --> 00:11:14.460
when strange things start to occur.&nbsp;
Left behind in the destruction and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.460 --> 00:11:19.440
debris are two blended families who do&nbsp;
everything they can to stay dry and keep&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.440 --> 00:11:23.400
their bodies from being snatched by the&nbsp;
unknown menace lurking in the wetlands.

00:11:23.400 --> 00:11:28.080
William Fichtner, who played the town's shifty&nbsp;
sheriff, told the Lansing State Journal that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.080 --> 00:11:32.820
they wanted to end Season 1 with a twist:&nbsp;
the sheriff's wife, who's presumed dead,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:32.820 --> 00:11:38.460
was the mastermind all along. Unfortunately,&nbsp;
casting took too long, leaving the series with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:38.460 --> 00:11:44.160
a different, jarring cliffhanger that never got&nbsp;
resolved. Despite desperate pleas from fans,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:44.160 --> 00:11:49.200
who even flew a banner over ABC's headquarters,&nbsp;
no one could undo the cancellation.

00:11:49.200 --> 00:11:54.840
When HBO's acclaimed series Rome fell&nbsp;
after two seasons, Scottish actor Kevin&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:54.840 --> 00:11:59.220
McKidd looked around for a new project.&nbsp;
There were plenty of generic cop dramas,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:59.220 --> 00:12:03.000
but he was taken by the script for&nbsp;
Journeyman. He told the VC Star,

00:12:03.000 --> 00:12:05.040
"I thought it was different and interesting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:05.040 --> 00:12:09.060
and really imaginative and cleverly&nbsp;
written. That's all you can go by."

00:12:09.060 --> 00:12:15.420
The 2007 NBC series, created by Kevin&nbsp;
Falls, centers around McKidd's Dan Vasser,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:15.420 --> 00:12:20.460
a San Francisco journalist who travels back&nbsp;
in time to right wrongs for others, while&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:20.460 --> 00:12:25.440
balancing the affections of his current flame&nbsp;
and his former partner, who is presumed dead.

00:12:25.440 --> 00:12:28.980
Journeyman was compared to both&nbsp;
The Time Traveller's Wife and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:28.980 --> 00:12:31.680
Quantum Leap. And Entertainment Weekly called it:

00:12:31.680 --> 00:12:37.740
"The awesome show the whole world is ignoring,&nbsp;
even though it's on right after Heroes."

00:12:37.740 --> 00:12:42.900
Lukewarm ratings and a lengthy writers strike&nbsp;
combined to kill off the series. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:42.900 --> 00:12:46.560
when asked about the series sudden ending&nbsp;
by Entertainment Weekly, Falls said,

00:12:46.560 --> 00:12:50.460
"We do answer quite a few questions so&nbsp;
fans will get a feeling of closure."

00:12:50.460 --> 00:12:54.840
Falls admitted to The Hollywood Reporter that&nbsp;
he felt bad about upending McKidd's life,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:12:54.840 --> 00:12:58.800
uprooting his family and moving them to&nbsp;
the United States. However, he added,

00:12:58.800 --> 00:13:03.180
"I'm relieved to say it turned into a pot&nbsp;
of gold. The next year [Kevin] started&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:03.180 --> 00:13:07.200
his first of 10 seasons on Grey's&nbsp;
Anatomy. He also learned to surf."

00:13:07.740 --> 00:13:11.520
When CBS rolled out its unique&nbsp;
and controversial reality series&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:11.520 --> 00:13:16.440
Kid Nation in 2007, there were a lot&nbsp;
of concerned parents. The only adult&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:16.440 --> 00:13:21.480
to be found in the ghost town of Bonanza&nbsp;
City, New Mexico was host Jonathan Karsh,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:21.480 --> 00:13:26.280
who stood on the sidelines and watched&nbsp;
as 40 kids fended for themselves for 40&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:26.280 --> 00:13:31.320
days. The idea was to form a community without&nbsp;
descending into Lord of the Flies territory.

00:13:31.320 --> 00:13:33.900
"Girls and boys of Bonanza City,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:37.440 --> 00:13:40.500
I'm sure we've all heard the term,&nbsp;
over and over again, 'Deal with it.'"

00:13:40.500 --> 00:13:45.180
Even before the show premiered, bad press&nbsp;
haunted Kid Nation, largely due to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:45.180 --> 00:13:49.920
agreement parents signed which prevented them&nbsp;
from suing even if their child died. Hollywood&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:49.920 --> 00:13:54.060
reporter Nikki Finke loudly pleaded for the&nbsp;
network to yank the show from their slate,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:13:54.060 --> 00:13:57.960
and advertisers were initially hesitant&nbsp;
to promote their brands alongside it.

00:13:57.960 --> 00:14:03.720
Regardless, CBS stood by Kid Nation defending&nbsp;
their care of the kids and even prepping a second&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:03.720 --> 00:14:08.820
season. The show premiered to 9.4 million&nbsp;
viewers, proving popular with youngsters,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:08.820 --> 00:14:13.560
but the numbers dipped by the end of the&nbsp;
13-episode run and Season 2 never came to be.

00:14:13.560 --> 00:14:17.160
The great thing about "Kid Nation" is&nbsp;
that it pushed its contestants to new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:17.160 --> 00:14:19.980
limits. Executive producer&nbsp;
Tom Forman told Variety,

00:14:19.980 --> 00:14:23.940
"The part that was amazing and&nbsp;
controversial and groundbreaking was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:23.940 --> 00:14:27.900
the social element and the documentary&nbsp;
element as the kids figured it out."

00:14:27.900 --> 00:14:31.080
Many of the kids have spoken fondly&nbsp;
about their time on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:31.080 --> 00:14:34.080
which is now looked on as a fascinating TV relic.

00:14:34.620 --> 00:14:38.880
The late writing duo Stan Burns and&nbsp;
Mike Marmer wrote for comedy greats&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:38.880 --> 00:14:42.780
like Milton Berle, Steve Allen, The&nbsp;
Smothers Brothers, and Carol Burnett,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:42.780 --> 00:14:47.700
but perhaps their greatest unsung contribution&nbsp;
to television was a show that saw them go&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:47.700 --> 00:14:52.740
completely ape. In an interview with the&nbsp;
Fort Worth Star-Telegram, Marmer recalled,

00:14:52.740 --> 00:14:57.060
"It all started with the mad idea of&nbsp;
making a comedy spy movie with no people,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:14:57.060 --> 00:15:01.860
just chimps. We knew nothing about chimps or&nbsp;
even whether such a project was possible."

00:15:01.860 --> 00:15:05.700
"Ooh. I just can't look. I can't believe my eyes!"

00:15:05.700 --> 00:15:10.380
With help from animal trainer Frank&nbsp;
Inn, a ten-minute demo film was made,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:10.380 --> 00:15:16.800
and audiences went absolutely bananas over it.&nbsp;
ABC opened their Saturday morning cages to the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:16.800 --> 00:15:21.780
concept that eventually became 1970's&nbsp;
The Lancelot Link, Secret Chimp Hour.

00:15:21.780 --> 00:15:26.160
The show centered on the titular&nbsp;
chimpanzee and his colleagues at A.P.E.:&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:26.160 --> 00:15:30.000
Mata Hairi and Commander Darwin. They&nbsp;
attempted to thwart the monkey business&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:30.000 --> 00:15:34.260
of C.H.U.M.P., with such rascally&nbsp;
characters as Baron von Butcher.

00:15:34.260 --> 00:15:40.080
"This is the first step in my brilliant plan to&nbsp;
steal and dispose of the Star of Karachi diamond!"

00:15:40.800 --> 00:15:47.100
The madness of watching these dressed-to-the-nines&nbsp;
chimps drive motorcycles, ski, and play tennis was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:47.100 --> 00:15:51.600
interspersed with small side gags called&nbsp;
"Chimpees," groovy musical interludes by&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:15:51.600 --> 00:15:55.200
the band The Evolution Revolution,&nbsp;
and even some Looney Tunes shorts.

00:15:55.200 --> 00:16:00.120
While Lancelot Link technically ran for&nbsp;
two seasons, the second was basically a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:00.120 --> 00:16:03.960
condensed version of the first. The&nbsp;
show would find a new generation of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:03.960 --> 00:16:09.300
fans when it naturally followed reruns of&nbsp;
The Monkees on Nick at Nite in the 1990s.

00:16:09.840 --> 00:16:14.760
Life's Too Short is a hilarious one-season&nbsp;
show from Ricky Gervais and Stephen&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:14.760 --> 00:16:18.720
Merchant. In a BBC press release,&nbsp;
Gervais described the show, saying,

00:16:18.720 --> 00:16:22.500
"Life's Too Short is [a] naturalist&nbsp;
observational comedy, dealing with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:22.500 --> 00:16:26.880
everyday problems, human foibles and&nbsp;
social faux pas. But with a dwarf."

00:16:26.880 --> 00:16:31.500
The lead role is played by Warwick Davis, who's&nbsp;
rarely been given the chance to show off his&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:31.500 --> 00:16:37.620
comedic side. Over seven episodes that began&nbsp;
airing in 2011, Davis raised his eyebrows and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:37.620 --> 00:16:43.680
rolled his eyes alongside stars such as Johnny&nbsp;
Depp, Liam Neeson, Sting, Helena Bonham Carter,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:16:43.680 --> 00:16:48.660
and Steve Carell, while being supported in&nbsp;
clueless fashion by Gervais and Merchant.

00:16:48.660 --> 00:16:52.800
"It's just really hard to act opposite&nbsp;
this, given the way it looks. I mean."

00:16:52.800 --> 00:16:54.060
"Yeah, okay."

00:16:54.060 --> 00:16:56.160
"I can't have its face staring at me like that."

00:16:56.160 --> 00:17:01.440
The comedy was filled with memorable moments,&nbsp;
and Gervais had a ball working with Davis.&nbsp;

00:17:01.440 --> 00:17:05.940
Davis told CNN that others in the&nbsp;
dwarf community appreciated it, saying,

00:17:05.940 --> 00:17:09.000
"[They] find that this character&nbsp;
doesn't paint a rosy picture,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:09.000 --> 00:17:11.760
but paints more of a real picture in a sense."

00:17:11.760 --> 00:17:14.760
"You're a dwarf. How can you&nbsp;
not know 'Hi ho, hi ho'?"

00:17:14.760 --> 00:17:17.100
Gervais said a second season was on deck,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:17.100 --> 00:17:22.320
but it turned into a special in which Davis was&nbsp;
reunited with his Willow co-star Val Kilmer.

00:17:22.320 --> 00:17:27.900
In the 1980s, NBC chief Brandon Tartikoff was&nbsp;
willing to take chances on shows that were a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:27.900 --> 00:17:32.520
bit off-kilter. While one of his pet projects,&nbsp;
Manimal, about a man who turns into animals,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:32.520 --> 00:17:36.960
didn't hit the mark, it didn't deter him&nbsp;
from trying similar things, like 1985's&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:36.960 --> 00:17:42.480
Misfits of Science. The show followed a crew of&nbsp;
extraordinary people who drove around in an ice&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:42.480 --> 00:17:47.640
cream truck. There was a scientist, a shrinking&nbsp;
man, an electrically charged Fonz-like guy,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:47.640 --> 00:17:52.560
and a telekinetic teen. Dean Paul Martin&nbsp;
and a young Courtney Cox were in the cast.

00:17:52.560 --> 00:17:58.500
The gang not only had to battle dastardly bad&nbsp;
guys weekly but also the ratings-superpower that&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:17:58.500 --> 00:18:04.500
was Dallas. The lackluster special effects made&nbsp;
the show cheesy yet endearing to young audiences,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:04.500 --> 00:18:09.660
who embraced it during its original run. The&nbsp;
plot seemed crazy for the time, but that was&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:09.660 --> 00:18:14.400
before Marvel took over Hollywood. In a piece&nbsp;
about the show, The Salt Lake City Tribune wrote,

00:18:14.400 --> 00:18:19.560
"It was about a bunch of people with super&nbsp;
powers, so maybe it was just ahead of its time."

00:18:20.280 --> 00:18:25.140
NBC's Outsourced is a TV adaptation of a&nbsp;
feature film which follows a group of call&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:25.140 --> 00:18:29.640
center workers in India. When Ken Kwapis, best&nbsp;
known for his work on The Office, saw the movie,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:29.640 --> 00:18:33.660
he asked the creators if he could pitch it&nbsp;
as a show. He told The Hollywood Reporter,

00:18:33.660 --> 00:18:37.800
"My pitch was very simple: Whether&nbsp;
it's a paper company in Scranton, Pa.,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:37.800 --> 00:18:42.780
or a call center in Mumbai, the petty&nbsp;
foibles of office life are universal."

00:18:42.780 --> 00:18:48.780
Clustered on a night of big NBC comedies&nbsp;
alongside The Office, 30 Rock, and Community,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:48.780 --> 00:18:53.580
Outsourced had a lot to live up to. And while&nbsp;
it didn't go down well with many critics,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:53.580 --> 00:18:59.280
those who liked it, raved about it. While some&nbsp;
said the show reinforced negative stereotypes,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:18:59.280 --> 00:19:02.520
writer Geetika Lizardi wrote&nbsp;
in a Los Angeles Times op-ed:

00:19:02.520 --> 00:19:05.400
"The humor ultimately comes&nbsp;
from a place of affection."

00:19:06.120 --> 00:19:10.560
Leslie Nielsen's The Naked Gun films are&nbsp;
considered to be some of the greatest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:10.560 --> 00:19:15.720
slapstick comedies of all time, but many people&nbsp;
don't realize that his detective character Frank&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:15.720 --> 00:19:20.400
Drebin was in a TV show called Police Squad!&nbsp;
first. While promoting the hit movie Airplane!,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:20.400 --> 00:19:25.200
comedic masterminds Jim Abrahams and&nbsp;
the Zucker brothers watched a police&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:25.200 --> 00:19:29.880
procedural show and knew immediately&nbsp;
that the genre was ripe for a parody.

00:19:29.880 --> 00:19:32.580
"We would've come earlier, but&nbsp;
your husband wasn't dead then."

00:19:32.580 --> 00:19:38.280
ABC gave the concept a six-episode trial,&nbsp;
seeing it as a possible replacement for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:38.280 --> 00:19:42.300
Mork &amp; Mindy. Nielsen needed little&nbsp;
convincing to sign on, saying,

00:19:42.300 --> 00:19:45.960
"I would have paid to be in this. I'm&nbsp;
on the same wavelength with these guys."

00:19:45.960 --> 00:19:51.060
Puns and double entendres abounded. The&nbsp;
weekly guest star was usually killed off in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:51.060 --> 00:19:55.320
the opening credits, and those who did survive&nbsp;
– including Dick Clark, Dr. Joyce Brothers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:19:55.320 --> 00:20:01.200
and Tommy Lasorda – sought professional advice&nbsp;
from an omniscient shoe shine man named Johnny.

00:20:01.200 --> 00:20:06.120
"Johnny, some kids on the show&nbsp;
yesterday mentioned a new kind of music.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:06.120 --> 00:20:08.760
Ska. You know anything about it?"

00:20:08.760 --> 00:20:11.220
Nielsen told the Minneapolis Star and Tribune,

00:20:11.220 --> 00:20:14.640
"If ever there was a show that&nbsp;
I thought was going to be a hit,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:14.640 --> 00:20:17.460
this was it. [...] People&nbsp;
didn't know how to look at it."

00:20:17.460 --> 00:20:21.300
One person who did get it was&nbsp;
Washington Post critic Tom Shales,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:21.300 --> 00:20:24.900
who called it "arguably the&nbsp;
best comedy show of the 1980s."

00:20:25.680 --> 00:20:30.480
Patrick McGoohan made his name starring in&nbsp;
the series Danger Man, released in the U.S.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:30.480 --> 00:20:35.040
as Secret Agent. When he tired of the show,&nbsp;
he quit and moved on to what has become one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:35.040 --> 00:20:40.200
of the most influential shows of the 1960s:&nbsp;
The Prisoner. This fascinating one-season&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:20:40.200 --> 00:20:45.764
British series follows an unnamed intelligence&nbsp;
agent being held captive in a creepy village.

00:20:45.764 --> 00:20:46.860
"Where's the police station?"

00:20:46.860 --> 00:20:48.123
"There isn't one."

00:20:48.123 --> 00:20:48.939
"Can I use your phone?"

00:20:48.939 --> 00:20:50.271
"We haven't got one."

00:20:50.271 --> 00:20:55.080
The show was a metaphor for society at&nbsp;
large. McGoohan said in a 1977 interview,

00:20:55.080 --> 00:21:00.000
"Your village may be different from other&nbsp;
people's villages but we are all prisoners."

00:21:00.000 --> 00:21:05.880
The mind-bending series, which launched the&nbsp;
catchphrase: "Be seeing you," was shot at the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:05.880 --> 00:21:11.460
Welsh seaside. It featured cryptic villagers&nbsp;
decked out in mod fashions and the creepiest&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:11.460 --> 00:21:16.200
ever use of a white meteorologist balloon.&nbsp;
It even captured the hearts of the Beatles,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:16.200 --> 00:21:19.380
so much so that they let the show use their song&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:19.380 --> 00:21:24.300
"All You Need is Love" in an episode.&nbsp;
George Harrison's son Dhani told Wired,

00:21:24.300 --> 00:21:29.220
"[It was] the only time a Beatles song&nbsp;
has ever been licensed to a TV show."

00:21:29.760 --> 00:21:34.680
The 2010 thriller series Rubicon&nbsp;
is an overlooked gem. Speaking to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:34.680 --> 00:21:37.260
The Baltimore Sun, writer Henry Bromell said that,

00:21:37.260 --> 00:21:42.180
"Rubicon was born out of the belief that&nbsp;
we in the United States could wake up one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:42.180 --> 00:21:46.740
day soon and find our democracy&nbsp;
gone, not vanquished by an army,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:46.740 --> 00:21:51.060
but by an almost invisible collusion&nbsp;
between business and government. I&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:21:51.060 --> 00:21:55.860
know I'm not the only one who feels helpless&nbsp;
and powerless in the face of this collusion."

00:21:55.860 --> 00:22:00.660
The show debuted to two million viewers,&nbsp;
which was a record for AMC at the time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:00.660 --> 00:22:06.180
but it was soon eclipsed by a juggernaut called&nbsp;
The Walking Dead. The numbers didn't hold up,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:06.180 --> 00:22:12.360
and on a channel where Mad Men and Breaking Bad&nbsp;
made good, Rubicon became the walking dead itself.

00:22:12.360 --> 00:22:15.600
In an interview with AV&nbsp;
Club, Bromell projected that

00:22:15.600 --> 00:22:21.180
"[James Badge Dale] won't have to be doing&nbsp;
as much staring and thinking next season."

00:22:21.180 --> 00:22:27.360
But it wasn't meant to be. Season 2 never&nbsp;
happened, and a great show was lost. In 2019, The&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:27.360 --> 00:22:32.880
New York Times revisited the show in an article&nbsp;
headlined: "Ahead of Its Time, Rubicon Still Holds&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:32.880 --> 00:22:39.060
Up." And Vulture's Vikram Murthi agreed in a piece&nbsp;
titled: "The Time Is Right to Rediscover Rubicon."

00:22:39.900 --> 00:22:45.120
Inspired by the Pirates of the Caribbean ride at&nbsp;
Disneyland, the 1982 show Voyagers! is about a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:45.120 --> 00:22:51.000
time traveler named Phineas Bogg, played by&nbsp;
Jon-Erik Hexum, and a wise-beyond-his-years orphan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:22:51.000 --> 00:22:56.880
named Jeffrey Jones, played by Meeno Peluce. It&nbsp;
spelled out its premise in its opening credits:

00:22:56.880 --> 00:23:00.780
"We travel through time to help history&nbsp;
along... give it a push where it's needed."

00:23:00.780 --> 00:23:04.740
While the show took artistic liberties&nbsp;
with actual historical events,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:04.740 --> 00:23:09.120
it was all in the name of pleasing the target&nbsp;
audience. Peluce told The Columbia Record,

00:23:09.120 --> 00:23:14.220
"In this show we roll up our sleeves,&nbsp;
dig right in and become part of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:14.220 --> 00:23:18.360
history. [...] It's every kid's fantasy to&nbsp;
participate in the great events in time."

00:23:18.360 --> 00:23:24.240
Weaving through the ages, they cross paths, and&nbsp;
sometimes swords, with Cleopatra, Abraham Lincoln,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:24.240 --> 00:23:30.660
Lawrence of Arabia, Thomas Edison, Harry Houdini,&nbsp;
Charles Lindbergh, Robin Hood, and Kublai Khan.

00:23:30.660 --> 00:23:35.340
The Washington Post hailed Voyagers!&nbsp;
as "a joy ride from start to finish,"&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:35.340 --> 00:23:40.500
and the Citizens' Voice praised the show&nbsp;
for its "attempts both to entertain and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:40.500 --> 00:23:44.820
educate our children." Tragically, Hexum&nbsp;
died following an on-set incident with a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:44.820 --> 00:23:49.980
prop gun in 1984. This meant that a second&nbsp;
season of Voyagers! could never happen,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:49.980 --> 00:23:54.480
even if the network had wanted to make it.&nbsp;
NBC had already pulled the plug, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:54.480 --> 00:23:58.980
on account of the show struggling to go up&nbsp;
against 60 Minutes, which aired at the same&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:23:58.980 --> 00:24:05.280
time. Voyagers! remains an '80s time capsule,&nbsp;
one that is definitely worth digging up.

